                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="707-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="707-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ><img id="707-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ></span>Red Microphones Type A</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Detachable-Capsule Tube Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Red Type A was manufactured by Blue Mics as one of a pair of low-cost microphone bodies that served as the foundation for a system of interchangeable capsules. The Type A was a tube condenser; the Type B was a FET condenser.</p><p>The tube was an ECC88; this is a dual-triode tube. The output circuit is transformerless; it uses 3 transistors (presumably one phase-splitter and two bipolars) to produce balanced output.</p><p><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/blue/TypeA-kit.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="Type A Kit" width="150" />As of May, 2007, pricing for the Type A was as follows:</p><ul><li>$1345 for the Type A body, no capsule</li><li>$649 for most of the Red capsules (R0-R7, not R4)</li><li>$849 for the R4, aka &#8220;The Big Omni,&#8221; described as a &#8220;small diaphragm sphere pressure omni&#8221; capsule</li></ul><p>The Red mic line was discontinued in 2008, and rebranded by Blue as the Stage 1 and Stage 2; the capsules are now called Bottle Rockets.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Red/Type-A">Red Microphones Type A</a> is also known as: typea.</p>
